# Prime Agent daemon systemd --user unit - WITHDRAWN for Deploy A. DO NOT USE.
#
# This template does NOT fit the daemon's restart model and is kept only to document why the
# supported path is the launcher transient scope (PRIME_AGENT_DAEMON_SCOPE=1 in
# cli/daemon-launch.ts) under the capped prime-agent.slice (see prime-agent.slice in this dir).
#
# Why a persistent Type=simple unit is wrong here:
#   1. Self-relaunch update-restart: the OLD supervisor spawns the NEW one and EXITS
#      (daemon-supervisor.ts). A Type=simple unit's main pid then exits, so systemd marks the
#      service dead and, with the default KillMode=control-group, KILLS the freshly spawned
#      successor and every worker/kernel. KillMode=process avoids the kill but the unit still
#      shows inactive after every update-restart, which is misleading and breaks Restart= logic.
#   2. MemoryOOMGroup= is systemd 247+ and is silently ignored on this host's systemd 239.
#   3. After=default.target together with WantedBy=default.target is an ordering cycle for a
#      user unit.
#
# Supported alternative (Deploy A):
#   - Install prime-agent.slice (capped parent slice) from this directory.
#   - Set PRIME_AGENT_DAEMON_SCOPE=1 in every launching shell (see prime-agent-daemon.env).
#   - The launcher wraps each daemon start in an auto-named transient scope under that slice; the
#     supervisor, workers, kernels, and uv helpers inherit the one capped cgroup, and a shell-
#     driven or self-relaunch restart re-enters the same slice cap without a unit-name collision.
#
# A persistent unit that fits this daemon (a launcher-owned scope, not Type=simple with
# self-relaunch) is Deploy B work; do not enable a .service for the daemon under Deploy A.
